  "narrative": "All estimates are interpreted as already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so positive values support the registered claim and negative values oppose it. I used approximately 10% practical thresholds from the absolute outcome means, for example KRAS about 0.0245, RIT1 about 0.0256, WRN about 0.0301, MDM2 about 0.0269, USP7 about 0.0093, and NFE2L2 about 0.0093. H13 remains accepted: MSI-high is associated with much stronger WRN dependency overall, discovery +0.790, interval +0.760 to +0.819, and independent validation +0.771, interval +0.720 to +0.822, far above the WRN threshold. H90 is rejected and closed: BRAF V600E among KRAS-wildtype cells had only borderline discovery for weaker RIT1 dependency, discovery +0.013, interval -0.021 to +0.047, and independent validation was near null and on the opposite side of the registered direction, validation -0.007, interval -0.056 to +0.041; although the wide adjusted interval still includes possible positive effects, the independent estimate provides no support for a clinically meaningful weaker-RIT1 effect. H49 is rejected and closed: KRAS mutation among TP53-intact cells was near null on validation for stronger KRAS dependency, validation +0.001, interval -0.028 to +0.030, with a point estimate far below the KRAS 10% threshold and a lower bound below zero; discovery alone was also below threshold. I reassessed H96 as an accepted in-dataset association: CDKN2A loss among TP53-intact cells is associated with much stronger MDM2 dependency, signed discovery +0.119, interval +0.079 to +0.158, well above the MDM2 threshold. This reverses the original H87 hypothesis and is the most informative new finding, but it remains active pending independent validation or technical robustness checks. Overall supported findings in the supplied dataset include SMARCA4 loss with much stronger SMARCA2 dependency, independently validated; TP53 loss with much weaker USP7 dependency, independently validated and supported outside the dominant Avana library; KEAP1 loss with much weaker NFE2L2 dependency, independently validated and supported outside Avana; and MSI-high with much stronger WRN dependency overall and in adenocarcinoma, with additional support in higher mutation-burden and BRCA2-intact contexts. Negative or unsupported findings include NF1-loss as a broad KRAS-wildtype bypass, because validation was null; BRAF V600E with meaningful BRAF dependency; KRAS mutation with stronger KRAS dependency in TP53-intact cells; BRAF V600E with weaker RIT1 dependency among KRAS-wildtype cells; ERBB2 amplification with meaningful ERBB2 or RIT1 dependency; KRAS mutation with weaker RIT1 dependency; PTEN loss with EGFR or KRAS-wildtype bypass; PIK3CA mutation with EGFR bypass; MSI-high with DHX9 dependency; BRCA2-loss synthetic-lethality patterns for WRN or DHX9; MYC amplification with POLR2A dependency; RB1 loss with KIF18A dependency; TP53 loss with RPL11 dependency; and STK11 loss with NFE2L2 dependency overall. Unresolved uncertainty remains for borderline claims such as EGFR amplification, broad KRAS mutation, TP53 loss with MDM2, ERBB2 amplification with STAT3, several broad STAT3 and MYC predictors, and the SMARCA4 batch interaction H35, where the lower confidence bound remains below the practical 10% benchmark. The most informative next analysis is independent validation or technical and subgroup robustness of H96, CDKN2A loss in TP53-intact cells with stronger MDM2 dependency, including the proposed non-Avana and adenocarcinoma checks. Additional useful follow-up is the proposed conditional STK11-loss test among KEAP1-intact cells for NFE2L2 and the PIK3CA-mutation test among KRAS-wildtype cells. All conclusions are associations within the supplied dataset, not causal claims or claims of external generalizability."
